--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/thingino-ipud/files/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,148 @@
+# thingino-ipud
+
+On-camera object detection on the Infinity6C IPU, the SoC's neural-network unit.
+
+## Pipeline
+
+```text
+SCL output port (model size, NV12) -> MI_IPU_Invoke -> YOLOv5 decode + NMS -> sinks
+```
+
+- **Frames.** raptor owns SCL device 0, channel 0, and configures ports 0-3 as
+  its streams need. ipud configures, enables and drains a port raptor left
+  unconfigured (the highest free one), at the model's input size, NV12, no
+  compression. A second process may do this on raptor's channel; the frames
+  arrive at the sensor rate and raptor's own ports are not disturbed. Ports 2
+  and 3 take at most 1920 wide. If raptor restarts, the port goes with its
+  channel; ipud notices and opens it again.
+- **Inference.** `libmi_ipu.so` from the SigmaStar drop, dlopened after its MI
+  dependencies, after `MI_SYS_Init(0)`. `CreateDevice` loads the IPU firmware,
+  `CreateCHN` the compiled model. `DevAttr.maxDynBufSize` must cover the model's
+  variable buffer; ipud reads it from the model header with
+  `MI_IPU_GetOfflineModeStaticInfo` (word 0 of a 92-byte info on this drop). The
+  model must be compiled for the runtime the drop carries: a model from an older
+  SGS release is refused at `CreateCHN` (error 54).
+- **Memory.** With an 800x480 model ipud takes 4.27 MB of the MMA heap at a cap
+  of 12 a second or less, 4.85 MB at full rate, all returned when it exits:
+  the model (913 KB), its variable buffer (1.66 MB), the output tensors
+  (532 KB) and the port's frames (576 KB each, two at depth 1, three at depth 2;
+  `-D`). Check the heap's free space
+  (`/proc/mi_modules/mi_sys_mma/mma_heap_name0`) before enabling it on a unit
+  with a small heap.
+- **Zero copy.** The model's input tensor is one NV12 plane. When the SCL frame is
+  packed (stride equal to the width, UV right after Y), ipud points the input
+  tensor at the frame's own buffer. On the first frame it runs both the copy and
+  the zero-copy path and keeps zero copy only if the outputs are byte-identical.
+  MI allocates the channel's input buffer at `CreateCHN` and keeps it until the
+  channel goes (`PutInputTensors` does not return it), so once zero copy has
+  passed ipud re-creates the channel with input depth 0, which allocates none,
+  checks the new channel on the same frame, and keeps it only if the outputs are
+  again byte-identical (576 KB less). A frame that is not packed later sends it
+  back to the copy path, with the buffer.
+- **Port depth.** One frame queued for ipud (depth 1, two frames in MI) keeps up
+  with a 10 a second cap; at full rate depth 1 holds ipud to about 15 a second,
+  depth 2 (three frames) keeps 24-25. ipud picks 1 for a cap of 12 or less.
+- **Decode.** Anchor-based YOLOv5 heads `[1, 3, H, W, 5 + nc]` of quantised
+  logits (`real = (q - zeroPnt) * scalar`), decoded the v6+ way
+  (`xy = (2s - 0.5 + grid) * stride`, `wh = (2s)^2 * anchor`, score =
+  `sig(obj) * sig(best class)`), the stock YOLOv5 anchors, then per-class NMS.
+
+## Facts found on hardware
+
+- `MI_IPU_Invoke` does not write the input tensor back from the CPU cache. After
+  the CPU fills the input, call `MI_SYS_FlushInvCache` on it before `Invoke`, or
+  the IPU reads part of the previous frame and the same input gives different
+  outputs on every call (`ipu_rt.c`, `cache_mode`).
+- `MI_SYS_ChnOutputPortGetBuf(port*, bufinfo*, handle*)` takes no SoC id and
+  copies out a 488-byte `MI_SYS_BufInfo_t`; `MI_SYS_GetFd(port*, int*)` takes
+  none either, `MI_SYS_SetChnOutputPortDepth` does. The frame fields' offsets in
+  that struct are in `scl_src.c`.
+- The output descriptor's `innerMost` is in bytes (22 for 11 INT16 channels).
+
+## Outputs
+
+Box coordinates are normalised to the full frame.
+
+- **Status file** `/run/ipud/status.json`, rewritten every 500 ms: rate, IPU time,
+  port, the latest objects. The web UI page and the preview overlay read it.
+- **Socket** `/run/ipud.sock`: one JSON line per inference to every client
+  (`ipucat` prints them), with the frame's MI pts, for local consumers that need
+  low latency.
+- **MQTT** (`ipud.mqtt`), with the broker of `/etc/send2.json`:
+  `<topic>/status` online/offline (retained, and the last will),
+  `<topic>/<label>` ON/OFF on presence change (retained, with a hold), and
+  `<topic>/detections` (the socket line) while something is detected, at most
+  every `mqtt_interval_ms`, and one line with no objects when the last one goes.
+  On every (re)connect ipud republishes each reported class's state, and on
+  exit it turns off what was on, so no retained ON outlives a run.
+- **Home Assistant** (`ipud.ha`): `ipud-ha` (in `/usr/libexec/`) publishes MQTT
+  discovery through thingino-ha's library for the camera's own HA device: a
+  `<Class> detected` occupancy sensor per reported class and an "Objects"
+  sensor (the count, the boxes as attributes). ipud then publishes on
+  thingino-ha's broker under `cameras/<hostname>/ipud/`, in place of
+  `ipud.mqtt`'s topic. What was published is listed in `/etc/ipud-ha.list`;
+  turning it off clears exactly that.
+- **Hook** (`ipud -x cmd`): `cmd <label> ON|OFF` on each presence change, with
+  `IPUD_SCORE` and `IPUD_DRAW` (the class's boxes, as Frigate's `draw` object)
+  in its environment.
+- **Frigate events** (`ipud.frigate`, `frigate_url`, `frigate_camera`): the hook
+  `ipud-frigate` opens a Frigate manual event while a class is present (sub
+  label `ipud`, the boxes drawn on its snapshot) and ends it after, through
+  Frigate's API on its internal port 5000.
+- **ONVIF events** (`ipud.onvif`): `ipud-onvif` (installed in `/usr/libexec/`)
+  adds one event per object group to `/etc/onvif.json`, and ipud keeps
+  `/run/motion/ipud_<group>` while any reported class of the group is present
+  (with the presence hold); `onvif_notify_server` raises the event while the
+  file exists. The groups and topics are person (`PeopleDetect`), vehicle
+  (bicycle, car, motorcycle, bus, truck: `VehicleDetect`) and pet (cat, dog:
+  `DogCatDetect`), all under `tns1:RuleEngine/MyRuleDetector/`, the set Home
+  Assistant's ONVIF integration turns into Person, Vehicle and Pet Detection
+  sensors. Only the groups of the reported classes are added. The ONVIF server
+  takes at most 8 events, relays included; a group that does not fit is skipped
+  with a warning rather than break the server's config.
+
+## Settings
+
+The `ipud` block of `/etc/thingino.json` (defaults in `ipud.json`); `S35ipud`
+reads it at start, and the web UI page writes it and restarts ipud.
+
+## Where the model comes from
+
+`ipud.source` picks it; `ipud-paths` (installed in `/usr/libexec/`) resolves it
+for both `S35ipud` and the web UI's CGI:
+
+| `source` | Model | IPU firmware |
+|---|---|---|
+| `builtin` (default) | `/usr/share/ipud/model.img` | `/usr/share/ipud/ipu_firmware.bin` |
+| `sdcard` | `<sd_dir>/<sd_model>`, the first `*.img` there when `sd_model` is empty; `sd_dir` defaults to `/mnt/mmcblk0p1/ipud` | `<sd_dir>/ipu_firmware.bin`, else the built-in one |
+| `custom` | `model` | `firmware`, else the built-in one |
+
+A config from before `source` that names its own `model` counts as `custom`.
+The class names come from a `<model>.labels` file beside the model (one
+comma-separated line) when there is one, else from `labels`, else ipud's
+default, the built-in model's six classes in COCO order:
+`person,bicycle,car,motorcycle,bus,truck`.
+
+The vendor files are never in this repository: no license lets us publish the
+IPU firmware or a vendor model. A private build stages them in this package's
+`vendor/` folder (git-ignored) before it builds, and the package installs them
+to `/usr/share/ipud/`; an image built without them has no built-in model, and
+the web UI page says so. New models, ours or a vendor's, go on the SD card with
+no rebuild: drop the `.img` (and a `.labels`) in the folder and pick it on the
+page.
+
+## Getting the vendor files
+
+An image built without the model can get it from firmware its user already
+has. `tools/ipu-extract/ipu_extract.py` of the SigmaStar forward-port
+repository (<https://github.com/andylee77/thingino-sigmastar-fwd>; MIT, no
+vendor bytes) takes the model out of the JL12D2's stock firmware (a full flash
+read of the camera, or its vendor firmware download) and checks it against the
+md5 ipud is known to run.
+
+No IPU firmware is needed: the runtime never reads the file ipud names (on an
+SSC378DE camera an empty and an all-zero file ran the model the same). ipud
+still checks that the file exists, so the tool writes an empty
+`ipu_firmware.bin` beside `model.img` and `model.labels`: copy the three into
+this package's `vendor/` before a build, or the folder onto the SD card as
+`ipud/` and set the source to `sdcard`.
diff --git a/package/thingino-ipud/files/S35ipud b/package/thingino-ipud/files/S35ipud
new file mode 100755
index 000000000..2ba2ead2e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/thingino-ipud/files/S35ipud
@@ -0,0 +1,182 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# S35ipud -- on-camera object detection on the Infinity6C IPU (thingino-ipud).
+# Settings: the "ipud" block of /etc/thingino.json. It starts only when
+# ipud.enabled is true and the model its source names is there: built in
+# (/usr/share/ipud, private images only), on the SD card, or a custom path
+# (/usr/libexec/ipud-paths resolves it). MQTT, when ipud.mqtt is true, uses the
+# broker of /etc/send2.json's "mqtt" block (send2mqtt's) and the topic
+# ipud.mqtt_topic (empty: thingino/<hostname>/ipud). Raptor's init script also
+# stops ipud before rvd goes down and starts it once rvd is back
+# (/etc/raptor.d/ipud). ipud.onvif adds ONVIF object events to /etc/onvif.json
+# (/usr/libexec/ipud-onvif) and has ipud keep their files in /run/motion.
+# ipud.ha puts ipud's entities on the camera's Home Assistant device
+# (/usr/libexec/ipud-ha, thingino-ha's broker) instead of ipud.mqtt's topic.
+# ipud.frigate opens a Frigate event while a class is present
+# (/usr/libexec/ipud-frigate; ipud.frigate_url, ipud.frigate_camera).
+
+DAEMON=/usr/sbin/ipud
+PIDFILE=/run/ipud.pid
+NAME=ipud
+SEND2=/etc/send2.json
+STATUS=/run/ipud/status.json
+ONVIF=/usr/libexec/ipud-onvif
+HA=/usr/libexec/ipud-ha
+FRIGATE=/usr/libexec/ipud-frigate
+EVENT_DIR=/run/motion
+DEFAULT_LABELS=person,bicycle,car,motorcycle,bus,truck
+
+. /usr/libexec/ipud-paths
+get() { ipud_get "$1"; }
+
+# Say why ipud is not running where the web UI looks.
+not_running() {
+	mkdir -p "${STATUS%/*}"
+	printf '{"running":false,"reason":"%s"}\n' "$1" >"$STATUS"
+}
+
+num() {
+	case "$1" in
+	"" | *[!0-9.]*) echo "$2" ;;
+	*) echo "$1" ;;
+	esac
+}
+
+# The ONVIF event groups (ipud-onvif) of the classes ipud reports: ipud.report,
+# or every class of the model when it is empty.
+onvif_groups() {
+	list=",${1:-$2},"
+	g=
+	case "$list" in *,person,*) g="$g person" ;; esac
+	case "$list" in *,bicycle,* | *,car,* | *,motorcycle,* | *,bus,* | *,truck,*) g="$g vehicle" ;; esac
+	case "$list" in *,cat,* | *,dog,*) g="$g pet" ;; esac
+	echo $g
+}
+
+start() {
+	[ -x "$DAEMON" ] || return 0
+	if [ "$(get enabled)" != "true" ]; then
+		[ -x "$ONVIF" ] && "$ONVIF" off
+		[ -x "$HA" ] && "$HA" off >/dev/null 2>&1 &
+		not_running "disabled"
+		return 0
+	fi
+	ipud_resolve
+	if [ -n "$IPUD_MISSING" ]; then
+		echo "$NAME: $IPUD_SOURCE model: $IPUD_MISSING" >&2
+		not_running "$(printf '%s' "$IPUD_MISSING" | tr -d '"\\')"
+		return 1
+	fi
+	if [ -f "$PIDFILE" ] && kill -0 "$(cat "$PIDFILE")" 2>/dev/null; then
+		echo "$NAME already running"
+		return 0
+	fi
+
+	set -- -f "$IPUD_FW" -m "$IPUD_MODEL" -Z -I 500 \
+		-c "$(num "$(get score)" 0.4)" -i "$(num "$(get iou)" 0.45)" \
+		-r "$(num "$(get max_fps)" 10)" -H "$(num "$(get hold_ms)" 3000)" \
+		-M "$(num "$(get mqtt_interval_ms)" 500)"
+	[ -n "$IPUD_LABELS" ] && set -- "$@" -L "$IPUD_LABELS"
+	report=$(get report)
+	[ -n "$report" ] && set -- "$@" -k "$report"
+
+	if [ -x "$ONVIF" ]; then
+		if [ "$(get onvif)" = "true" ]; then
+			# shellcheck disable=SC2046
+			"$ONVIF" on $(onvif_groups "$report" "${IPUD_LABELS:-$DEFAULT_LABELS}")
+			set -- "$@" -O "$EVENT_DIR"
+		else
+			"$ONVIF" off
+		fi
+	fi
+
+	# Home Assistant: thingino-ha's broker and the camera's device; it wins
+	# over ipud.mqtt. Discovery goes in the background: at boot S35 runs
+	# before the network, and the retained discovery of an earlier start
+	# stands meanwhile.
+	ha_on=
+	if [ -x "$HA" ] && [ "$(get ha)" = "true" ]; then
+		if ha_env=$("$HA" env) && eval "$ha_env"; then
+			export IPUD_MQTT_HOST IPUD_MQTT_PORT IPUD_MQTT_USER IPUD_MQTT_PASS IPUD_MQTT_CLIENT_ID
+			"$HA" on "$report" "${IPUD_LABELS:-$DEFAULT_LABELS}" >/dev/null 2>&1 &
+			set -- "$@" -q "$IPUD_HA_TOPIC"
+			ha_on=1
+		fi
+	elif [ -x "$HA" ]; then
+		"$HA" off >/dev/null 2>&1 &
+	fi
+
+	# Frigate: a manual event per class while it is present (ipud -x).
+	# The reset ends events a previous run left open (none after a boot:
+	# their ids live in /run).
+	if [ -x "$FRIGATE" ] && [ "$(get frigate)" = "true" ]; then
+		IPUD_FRIGATE_URL=$(get frigate_url)
+		IPUD_FRIGATE_CAMERA=$(get frigate_camera)
+		if [ -n "$IPUD_FRIGATE_URL" ] && [ -n "$IPUD_FRIGATE_CAMERA" ]; then
+			export IPUD_FRIGATE_URL IPUD_FRIGATE_CAMERA
+			"$FRIGATE" reset
+			set -- "$@" -x "$FRIGATE"
+		else
+			echo "$NAME: ipud.frigate is on, but frigate_url or frigate_camera is empty" >&2
+		fi
+	fi
+
+	if [ -z "$ha_on" ] && [ "$(get mqtt)" = "true" ] && [ -f "$SEND2" ]; then
+		IPUD_MQTT_HOST=$(jct "$SEND2" get mqtt.host 2>/dev/null)
+		IPUD_MQTT_PORT=$(jct "$SEND2" get mqtt.port 2>/dev/null)
+		IPUD_MQTT_USER=$(jct "$SEND2" get mqtt.username 2>/dev/null)
+		IPUD_MQTT_PASS=$(jct "$SEND2" get mqtt.password 2>/dev/null)
+		IPUD_MQTT_CLIENT_ID=$(jct "$SEND2" get mqtt.client_id 2>/dev/null)
+		export IPUD_MQTT_HOST IPUD_MQTT_PORT IPUD_MQTT_USER IPUD_MQTT_PASS IPUD_MQTT_CLIENT_ID
+		topic=$(get mqtt_topic)
+		[ -n "$topic" ] || topic="thingino/$(hostname)/ipud"
+		if [ -n "$IPUD_MQTT_HOST" ]; then
+			set -- "$@" -q "$topic"
+		else
+			echo "$NAME: ipud.mqtt is on but $SEND2 names no broker" >&2
+		fi
+	fi
+
+	echo "Starting $NAME..."
+	start-stop-daemon -S -b -m -p "$PIDFILE" -x "$DAEMON" -- "$@"
+}
+
+# Waits for the exit: the raptor hook needs ipud off the SCL channel before rvd
+# stops. ipud disables its port and leaves MI on SIGTERM.
+stop() {
+	pid=$(cat "$PIDFILE" 2>/dev/null)
+	[ -n "$pid" ] || pid=$(pidof ipud)
+	rm -f "$PIDFILE"
+	[ -n "$pid" ] || return 0
+	echo "Stopping $NAME..."
+	kill $pid 2>/dev/null
+	i=0
+	while [ $i -lt 30 ] && kill -0 $pid 2>/dev/null; do
+		usleep 100000
+		i=$((i + 1))
+	done
+	if kill -0 $pid 2>/dev/null; then
+		kill -9 $pid 2>/dev/null
+	fi
+	# ipud clears its event files on exit; a killed one leaves them
+	rm -f "$EVENT_DIR/ipud_person" "$EVENT_DIR/ipud_vehicle" "$EVENT_DIR/ipud_pet"
+	return 0
+}
+
+case "$1" in
+	start)
+		start
+		;;
+	stop)
+		stop
+		;;
+	restart)
+		stop
+		start
+		;;
+	*)
+		echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}" >&2
+		exit 1
+		;;
+esac
+
+exit 0
